A chatbot handling travel queries presents real privacy concerns: user messages describing origin, destination, travel dates, and budget are inherently location-sensitive data. In our implementation, these messages are forwarded to Groq's API (a third-party LLM provider), meaning user input leaves our infrastructure. To mitigate exposure elsewhere, we made a deliberate architectural choice to use mock data for BlaBlaCar rides and hostels — since those APIs either require partner approval or don't offer public access, no user query data is ever sent to those third parties. Chat history is capped at the last 10 messages and is never persisted beyond the browser session, meaning no personal data is stored server-side. From a legal standpoint, a production version of this application would require a clear privacy policy disclosing that messages are processed by Groq, user consent for that processing, and GDPR-compliant data handling if deployed in Europe — including the right to erasure, which is straightforward given no server-side storage currently exists.